  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 234 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nOn Friday, an upper level low-pressure system over the Middle\nRockies will be sliding towards the east over northern CONUS\nthat will help bring in a cold front overnight and into Saturday\nmorning. Sunday onward, there is some uncertainty about the \nupper-level ridging over the U.S. and Canadian border. On \nMonday, guidance is still uncertain with the ridging to the \nnorth, but there is consistency that embedded shortwave troughs\nwould be going through the area. This is forecast to bring\ncooler temperatures and slight chances for showers. Temperatures\nFriday will be the warmest during the period with afternoon\nhighs being in the mid to upper 80s. From Saturday onward,\ntemperatures are forecast to be in the 70s as the cold front\nmoves in and a possible secondary cold front Monday. There is\nmedium confidence (40-55%) that temperatures would remain in the\n70s.\n\nShowers are also possible with the uncertainty of the upper-\nlevel pattern. As the cold front moves through Friday night and\ninto Saturday morning, there is high confidence (75%) for\novernight rain. However, there is some uncertainty with the\ntiming of the cold front as deterministic guidance shows the\nfront being further west than ensemble guidance. \n\nOn Sunday morning, with the confidence in the upper-level\npattern starting to deteriorate, confidence for showers also\nbegins to decrease heavily (75% to 30%) and would likely occur\nin the early morning hours. On Monday, there could be some\nshowers favoring south of I-70 as possible embedded shortwave\ntroughs could move through the area.",
